	url = {https://www.persee.fr/doc/ccmed_0007-9731_2001_num_44_175_2803},
	doi = {10.3406/ccmed.2001.2803},
	abstract = {Recounting the treatment of the so-called Ordo prophetarum in both monumental sculpture and texts of that drama itself, the author notes the emphasis on the surviving French texts even though more monuments depicting the theme survive in Italy. Examining the prophet cycles at the cathedrals of Cremona, Ferrara, Piacenza and Verona, the author argues that they vary among themselves, are not directly related to Quodvultdeus's sermon on which the Ordo prophetarum is based, and are not congruent with any of the surviving texts of the Ordo prophetarum . Instead, it is posited that there was an independant Italian medieval prophet tradition that originated at an early date and that many of the inscriptions on the scrolls held by the Italian sculpted prophets have their source in liturgical antiphons.},
